cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below, and also cite the inputs it rests on (see README.md, Citations)."
type: software
title: "Vanishing of Fourier coefficients from a uniform zero-free half-plane, and an uncertainty principle for involutions in PGL₂(𝔽_ℓ)"
version: 1.1.2
date-released: "2026-10-14"  # must match the date of the GitHub release (and .zenodo.json publication_date)
authors:
  - family-names: Blem
    given-names: Morgan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-7121-5989"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SlackEight/tau-vanishing"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A mathematical note (preprint) and a Lean 4 formalisation. Write Z(θ) for the statement that every Dedekind zeta
  function is nonzero in Re s > θ, uniformly in the field; Cureton and Thorner state Z(1 − 1/6000) in a preprint,
  and their Lean development (AxiomMath/DedekindZetaZeroFree, commit 09afbb4c; proofs generated by AxiomProver)
  proves Z(31/32), a constant that is not in the preprint (v1). The note records the transfer of the Serre / Murty–Murty–Saradha / Zywina argument to this
  setting: for a non-CM newform f with integer coefficients, Z(θ) implies #{p ≤ x : a_f(p) = 0} ≪ x^{(1+θ)/2}, so
  #{p ≤ x : τ(p) = 0} ≪ x^{63/64} given the Lean theorem. This implication is verified in Lean 4 in the form
  x^{(1+θ)/2+ε}, conditional on an explicit hypothesis encoding the mod-ℓ Galois representations and their large
  image (Deligne, Ribet–Momose, Swinnerton-Dyer), which is not formalised. The main new ingredient is an exact
  inequality for the involutions of PGL₂(𝔽_ℓ): every nonnegative class-function majorant φ satisfies μ(φ)λ_A(φ) ≥ κ_ℓ ≥ 1 − 1/ℓ
  (λ_A Bellaïche's Littlewood norm); it gives lower bounds, at the exponent (1+θ)/2, for three explicitly defined
  error functionals (single-ℓ majorants, single-extension Chebotarev certificates over fields whose Galois group maps
  onto ∏ PGL₂(𝔽_ℓ) with central kernel, per-prime Selberg sieves with pairwise error accounting). The repository also contains the scripts and output logs of the finite computations
  cited in the note. The note was prepared with substantial AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic, run as agents under
  the author's direction); the author takes responsibility for it and is verifying the proofs. It has not been
  refereed.
keywords:
  - Ramanujan tau function
  - Lehmer's conjecture
  - vanishing of Fourier coefficients
  - modular forms
  - Chebotarev density theorem
  - Dedekind zeta function
  - zero-free region
  - Littlewood norm
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
  - Mathlib
